31,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"It appears our development budget is tighter than usual, as it now covers floor fitting. Furthermore, we only generate revenue from the regional fund for sessions held on-site.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Shion,,"My findings are sobering. A call-back trial significantly reduced readmissions for the elderly, yet it was simply abandoned. Meanwhile, a joint clinic collapsed because of petty disputes over rotas and budgets. We are prone to wasting success.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I'm worried about our people. Ward nursing vacancies are rising and agency costs are climbing, though interestingly, the midwifery rota seems to have some unusual slack right now.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Everything is so crowded! The car park fills up by eleven, and the shuttle only reaches a few villages. Plus, the booking system is missing a lot of the work that doesn't have a fixed appointment!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It is a bit worrying, isn't it? I found that our agreements with outside surgeries take a very long time to sign, and our shared nursing rounds often struggle with simple things like locked gates. We must be careful.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shion, it is a shame to see success abandoned. Given the budget cuts I found, we must ensure our next choice is financially sustainable and doesn't rely on external funding that isn't there.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, sustainability is a matter of will as much as money. If we cannot even maintain a proven reduction in readmissions, a larger budget won't save a poorly managed program. We need a project that is structurally simple.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I agree that simplicity is key. If we choose something that puts too much pressure on our already struggling ward nurses, it might fail regardless of the budget. We should look for a balance.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, I totally agree! But we also have to think about the physical space. If the car park is already full by eleven, adding more people coming in will just make everything seize up!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Kenji, you are quite right to worry about the parking. It would be such a shame for a lovely program to be hindered by something as simple as a full car park or a locked gate. We must be thoughtful.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shizue, your point about locked gates suggests that any program relying on off-site visits may be inefficient. Combined with the lack of tariffs for off-site work, on-site programs seem more viable.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, your logic is sound. If we avoid the logistical friction of off-site visits and the bureaucratic nightmare of joint rotas, we minimize the chance of another quiet failure. On-site and independent is the way.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Jonathan, I agree that on-site is safer. However, we must also consider which teams can actually handle the work. If we can utilize the slack in midwifery, we might avoid burning out the ward nurses.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, that's a great idea! If we use the teams that have some breathing room, it'll be much better. But we still need to make sure the booking system can actually track it!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, your kindness toward the staff is wonderful. If we can find a way to help the patients without adding to the nurses' burdens, everyone will be much happier. Let us hope the candidates allow for that.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. We should seek a candidate that is on-site to secure funding, utilizes available staff capacity, and avoids the logistical bottlenecks Kenji mentioned. Let us see which proposals align with these constraints.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,4,talk,Shion,,I concur. We must prioritize a program that is not only fiscally viable but also operationally lean. I have no interest in seeing another effective trial discarded due to administrative incompetence.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Midori,,I hope we can find a project that brings a positive change to the hospital without overwhelming our colleagues. A balanced approach will be the most sustainable for everyone involved.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"I'm with you all! As long as we pick something that doesn't make the car park a nightmare or crash the booking system, I'm happy. Let's see what the options are!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Shizue,,I agree with everyone. It is so heartening to see us all looking out for one another and our patients. I shall look forward to seeing the candidates with a hopeful heart.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,3,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
52,3,,status,Shizue,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
53,3,,status,Shion,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
54,3,,status,Kenji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
55,3,,status,Midori,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"I lean toward Candidate B. It is on-site, which secures the regional fund tariffs and avoids the off-site logistical issues Shizue and I discussed. It also avoids the external surgery agreements that Shizue found problematic.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Shion,,"Candidate B is the most logical choice. It avoids the off-site friction of A and D, and unlike C, it doesn't risk the budget and rota disputes that killed our previous joint clinic. It is structurally the safest.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Midori,,"I am torn. While B is on-site, it requires physical therapists, and my review showed we are losing them to city posts. Candidate C, however, could utilize the slack in the midwifery rota.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"I'm worried about B! A dedicated space for rehab sounds great, but our outpatient rooms are already full by mid-morning and the car park is a mess. C seems better since it's scheduled and uses the midwifery slack!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"I feel for our dear midwives, and Candidate C sounds like a lovely way to use their time. However, I worry that a joint service between two departments might lead to the same disagreements Shion mentioned. Perhaps B is safer?","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, if therapy staffing is a risk, B might be unstable. However, C is a joint venture, which Shion warned us about. @Kenji, would C's scheduled nature actually ease the parking pressure compared to B?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Shion"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Kenji, scheduling does not magically create parking spaces. If C brings in a steady stream of new parents, the congestion remains. B is a concentrated risk, but C is a bureaucratic one. I prefer the risk we can manage.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I understand your concern about bureaucracy, but the risk of burnout is just as real. If we push B onto a therapy team that is already shrinking, we are setting them up for failure. C feels more sustainable for the staff.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Shion, you're right that more people means more cars, but C is for new parents who might come in fewer, longer visits! B is a whole unit, which sounds like it would just jam the booking system and the car park even more!","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, your concern for the staff is so touching. But I cannot help but worry about Candidate C; if the midwifery and pediatric departments cannot agree on the details, it would be such a pity to see it fall apart.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Kenji, if B creates a larger bottleneck in the car park and booking system, it may be less viable than we first thought. However, A and D are off-site and thus financially risky. Between B and C, we must weigh staffing against bureaucracy.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, the choice is simple. Staffing gaps can be filled with budget, but a culture of departmental infighting is a systemic rot. Candidate C is a repeat of our past failures. B is the only option that doesn't invite administrative collapse.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I understand your fear of infighting, but we cannot ignore the reality of our staffing. If we choose B, we are relying on a therapy team that is already depleted. That is a different kind of systemic rot—burnout. C is a chance to support our midwives.","{""words"":47,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, I'm with you! If the therapy team is already tired, B will just be a disaster. C uses the slack we actually have, and since it's for parents, it's a much more positive way to use our space!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Kenji, you always have such a positive outlook! It is true that supporting new parents is a wonderful goal. But I must be honest; my heart sinks thinking about those long months spent waiting for signatures and agreements. Candidate D would be so difficult.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,"Given the budget cuts and the need for on-site revenue, A and D are out. While B is financially sound, the staffing and parking risks are significant. C utilizes available capacity and is on-site. I vote for Candidate C.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,4,talk,Shion,,I cannot support C. It is a blueprint for the same failure we saw with the joint clinic. I will not vote for a project that invites a budget war. I vote for Candidate B.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,4,talk,Midori,,I believe we must prioritize the well-being of our staff to ensure the program's longevity. Candidate C is the only option that aligns with our current capacity without risking further burnout. I vote for Candidate C.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,4,talk,Kenji,,"I'm voting for Candidate C! It uses the staff who actually have time, and it's way better than adding a whole new unit that would just jam up the car park and the booking system. Candidate C it is!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"It is so difficult to choose when everyone has such good reasons. I worry about the disagreements Shion mentioned, but I cannot ignore the kindness Midori shows our staff. I shall vote for Candidate C.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
